{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789505114,"nonce":"mU21j1W9LYRXc5wA9yhhbxtaISM=","title":"Python: a builtin method read off a dynamic value raises AttributeError","message":"`zs = xs[0]` with `xs` a list of lists gives `zs` the dynamic type, and `app = zs.append` goes through `py$getattr$append`, which knows the program's classes and nothing else: AttributeError 'list' object has no attribute 'append', where CPython hands back the bound method. Calling the method directly (`zs.append(1)`) works, since the dynamic method call dispatches on the box's tag.\n\nThe getattr dispatcher needs the same tag dispatch for the methods of list, str, dict and set, producing the bound-method record the typed path produces (`bound_method` in lower.rs), so a method read off a dynamic value is a value like any other.","files":null}]}